# Implementation Plan

Build a native Android question-attempt screen using Kotlin and Jetpack Compose.

Work incrementally. Complete and verify each phase before moving to the next. Do not build the entire application in one shot.

==================================================
PHASE 0 — INSPECT
==================================================

Before writing code:

1. Inspect the existing Android project.
2. Inspect the actual data.json structure.
3. Identify:
   - root structure
   - subjects
   - chapters
   - questions
   - question types
   - question fields
   - option fields
   - metadata fields
   - image fields
   - video-solution fields
4. Identify the existing Gradle/Kotlin/Compose setup.

Do not assume the JSON structure. Use the actual file.

Do not modify the project during this phase.

Report what you found, then proceed.

==================================================
PHASE 1 — PROJECT STRUCTURE
==================================================

Use a simple clean structure:

data/
    local/
    mapper/

domain/
    model/
    repository/

presentation/
    question/
        QuestionScreen
        QuestionViewModel
        QuestionUiState
        QuestionHeader
        QuestionContent
        OptionCard
        BottomActions

rendering/
    MathContentWebView

Keep responsibilities separated.

Do not over-engineer.

Do not introduce unnecessary:
- Retrofit
- Room
- backend
- authentication
- multi-module architecture
- complex dependency injection

==================================================
PHASE 2 — LOAD JSON
==================================================

Bundle data.json inside:

app/src/main/assets/data.json

Create serialization models matching the actual JSON.

Keep JSON models separate from domain models.

Create domain models containing the information required by the UI.

Preserve:

subject
    -> chapter
        -> question
            -> options

Map the subject and chapter context into each question.

Flatten the questions into a List<Question> while preserving the original JSON order.

Parse the JSON off the main thread.

Handle malformed/missing JSON gracefully.

The app must show an error state instead of crashing if the JSON cannot be loaded.

Verify that real questions from data.json can be loaded before continuing.

==================================================
PHASE 3 — QUESTION TYPES
==================================================

Parse all question types present in the JSON.

Support:

singleCorrect
multipleCorrect
numerical

For the initial implementation, filter the question flow to singleCorrect questions.

Do not hardcode which questions are displayed.

Do not hardcode question IDs, option IDs, answers, chapters, subjects or metadata.

Keep the other question types available for later implementation.

==================================================
PHASE 4 — STATE MODEL
==================================================

Keep UI state separate from the raw JSON/domain models.

Create per-question answer state.

Example:

AnswerState(
    selectedOptionIds: Set<String>,
    checked: Boolean
)

Store state using question ID as the key.

Do NOT use the array index as the answer-state key.

The ViewModel should own:

- current question index
- question list
- answer states
- selected option
- checked state
- navigation
- answer validation

The Composables should only render state and emit user actions.

==================================================
PHASE 5 — SINGLE CORRECT LOGIC
==================================================

Implement four states.

1. Unanswered

selected = none
checked = false

All options neutral.

Check Answer disabled.

2. Selected

selected = one option
checked = false

Selected option highlighted.

Check Answer enabled.

3. Checked + correct

selected option is correct
checked = true

Selected option becomes green.

Show correct feedback.

4. Checked + wrong

selected option is incorrect
checked = true

Selected option becomes red.

Correct option becomes green.

Do NOT reveal the correct answer before Check Answer is pressed.

For singleCorrect, selecting another option must replace the previous selection.

Keep answer-checking logic outside the UI.

Create unit tests for:

- correct option
- incorrect option
- invalid option

==================================================
PHASE 6 — SCREEN UI
==================================================

Build the screen in Compose.

Structure:

Top bar
    Back
    Exam > Chapter

Scrollable content
    Question number / total
    Previous-year/source metadata
    Video solution indicator
    Question content
    Options

Fixed bottom action area
    Previous
    Check Answer
    Next

Only the question content should scroll.

The bottom action area must remain usable while question content is long.

Do not recreate the device status bar shown in the reference.

==================================================
PHASE 7 — QUESTION METADATA
==================================================

Read everything dynamically from the JSON.

Display when available:

- exam title
- chapter title
- current question number
- total question count
- previous-year/source information
- video solution indicator

Question number:

currentIndex + 1

Total:

questions.size

Do not hardcode these values.

If optional metadata is null/missing:

- hide that element
- never display "null"
- never crash

Do not implement video playback.

==================================================
PHASE 8 — RICH CONTENT RENDERER
==================================================

Question and option content can contain:

- plain text
- HTML
- HTML entities
- paragraphs
- bold text
- line breaks
- simple tables
- LaTeX
- MathML
- inline images
- image-heavy content
- mixed combinations

Create one reusable rich-content renderer.

Use:

Compose
    -> AndroidView
        -> WebView
            -> local HTML
                -> MathJax

The whole application must NOT be implemented as a WebView.

Only question/option rich content should use WebView.

==================================================
PHASE 9 — MATHJAX
==================================================

Bundle MathJax with the application.

Do not depend on a public MathJax CDN.

The renderer must support LaTeX and MathML.

Examples:

$...$

and:

<math>...</math>

Do not strip mathematical markup.

Do not convert math into plain text.

Do not replace mathematical expressions with placeholders.

Ensure MathJax typesetting completes correctly before treating the content as rendered.

==================================================
PHASE 10 — HTML + IMAGES
==================================================

Normal HTML should render correctly.

Support:

- paragraphs
- strong/bold
- line breaks
- spans
- entities
- simple tables

Support images from:

1. dedicated image fields
2. <img src="..."> inside HTML

Images should scale to the available width.

Remote image loading must fail gracefully.

A broken/slow image must never crash the screen.

==================================================
PHASE 11 — THEME
==================================================

Implement both light and dark themes.

Every required state must work in both themes:

Light:
- unanswered
- selected
- correct
- wrong

Dark:
- unanswered
- selected
- correct
- wrong

Use Compose Material theme for the native UI.

Pass the current theme into the rich-content renderer so HTML and math content also have appropriate colors/backgrounds.

Do not create separate screen implementations for light and dark mode.

==================================================
PHASE 12 — NAVIGATION
==================================================

Implement:

Previous
Next

First question:

Previous disabled.

Last question:

Next disabled.

Do not wrap around.

Navigation must preserve the original question order.

Verify navigation across questions from different chapters and subjects.

==================================================
PHASE 13 — STATE RESTORATION
==================================================

Test this exact sequence:

Question 1:
select B
do not check

Next

Question 2:
select C
check

Previous

Question 1:
B is still selected
Question 1 is still unchecked

Next

Question 2:
C is still selected
Question 2 is still checked

Also test screen recreation/rotation.

The current question and answer state must survive normal configuration changes.

Use ViewModel.

Use SavedStateHandle where appropriate for stronger restoration.

==================================================
PHASE 14 — ERROR HANDLING
==================================================

Gracefully handle:

- malformed JSON
- missing fields
- null metadata
- empty options
- unsupported question types
- invalid image URLs
- image loading failure
- WebView rendering failure
- unexpected HTML markup

Fatal data-loading error:

Show a simple error screen.

Individual rendering problems:

Preserve as much of the question as possible instead of crashing.

